- /*
-  * OTTERNET lab firewall - vSRX profile
-  *
-  * The one firewall in this lab. Four zones, and every tenant, customer and
-  * site reaches every other one by being routed out of the border leaf into a
-  * zone here and back in -- so there is no path between any two of those
-  * networks that does not cross this policy.
-  *
-  *   ge-0/0/0  10.250.110.2/30  <-> border-leaf1 Et10 (10.250.110.1)  zone k8s-prod
-  *   ge-0/0/1  10.250.210.2/30  <-> border-leaf1 Et11 (10.250.210.1)  zone app-prod
-  *   ge-0/0/2  10.250.150.2/30  <-> border-leaf1 Et14 (10.250.150.1)  zone wan
-  *   ge-0/0/3  10.250.170.2/30  <-> border-leaf1 Et15 (10.250.170.1)  zone branch
-  *   ge-0/0/4  10.250.10.2/30   <-> border-leaf1 Et16 (10.250.10.1)   zone acme-cloud
-  *   ge-0/0/5  10.250.20.2/30   <-> border-leaf1 Et17 (10.250.20.1)   zone globex-cloud
-  *   fxp0                           containerlab management            (vrnetlab)
-  *
-  * The last two are per-TENANT rather than per-function, and that is the point:
-  * each WAN tenant's cloud instances live in their own VRF in the fabric, so
-  * each needs its own handoff and its own zone. A tenant reaches its own cloud
-  * and no other, and the rule that says so names two zones rather than
-  * enumerating addresses.
-  *
-  * Interface naming matters: for the juniper_vsrx kind containerlab wires
-  * fw1:eth1 to ge-0/0/0, because fxp0 takes eth0 as management. A policy written
-  * against the wrong interface commits cleanly and never matches a packet.
-  *
-  * THIS FILE IS APPENDED TO vrnetlab's init.conf, not loaded instead of it.
-  * launch.py does `cat init.conf $STARTUP_CONFIG_FILE >> juniper.conf`, so
-  * everything in init.conf -- the admin user, fxp0, the mgmt_junos routing
-  * instance, ssh and netconf -- is already present and must NOT be repeated
-  * here. Redefining `system` or `routing-instances` fights with it.
-  *
-  * There is no policy budget to manage here. Junos licenses throughput and
-  * feature bundles (IPS, AppSecure, Content Security), not object counts, so the
-  * rules below are written one-per-intent rather than merged to fit under a
-  * ceiling, and the anti-spoofing deny is repeated as the first policy of each
-  * zone pair instead of being a single global rule.
-  */
- /*
-  * The admin password, set properly.
-  *
-  * vrnetlab's init.conf writes it as `plain-text-password-value "admin@123"`,
-  * and Junos does NOT hash that when the file is the boot configuration -- it
-  * stores the leaf verbatim, `show configuration system login` shows
-  * plain-text-password-value rather than the encrypted-password you would
-  * normally see after a commit, and the account has no usable credential. The
-  * commit succeeds, every other part of the config applies, and the only symptom
-  * is "Login incorrect" on a box that otherwise looks perfectly healthy.
-  *
-  * Repeating the `system` stanza here is therefore deliberate, and it is the one
-  * thing in this file that intentionally overlaps init.conf. The hash below is
-  * SHA-512 of the same password vrnetlab and containerlab already use, so
-  * `make fw-console`, clab's own tooling and the access controller all keep
-  * working with one credential:
-  *
-  *     openssl passwd -6 -salt otternetlab 'admin@123'
-  */
